data.content:
Since `TimeProvider` comes from DI but your class is `static`, you can't inject it. You have two clean options:

### Option 1 — Drop `static`, use constructor injection (recommended)
```csharp
public class TimestampFormatter(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string Now() => timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```
Register it in DI and inject where needed. This is the idiomatic approach.

### Option 2 — Keep `static`, accept `TimeProvider` as a parameter
```csharp
public static class TimestampFormatter
{
    public static string Now(TimeProvider timeProvider) =>
        timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```
Callers pass the `TimeProvider` they already have from DI.

---

**Option 1 is strongly preferred** — it keeps the DI dependency explicit, avoids threading `TimeProvider` through every call site manually, and makes the class easy to test. Want me to implement one of these?
